From: Andrew Murgola <andy@murgo.la>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ACPI: processor: take the N1x's firmware-disabled platform _LPI states

The Lenovo Yoga 9 2-in-1 16N1X11 (83N7, NVIDIA N1x) describes its idle
states as a four-level _LPI hierarchy: CPU (WFI, CoreIdle-OFF), cluster
(PlatIdle-DSUOFF), package (PlatIdle-PGKSL, PGKLL) and system
(Standby-DRIPS). Every container level's _LPI clears the Enabled flag of
its states unless \_SB.PEPD.ELST(1) has set MPEL, which only Windows'
platform extension driver calls. flatten_lpi_states() skips disabled
states, so Linux ends up with two states, WFI and core-off, and
suspend-to-idle never gets below core-off: the DSU, the fabric and DRAM
stay up and the laptop draws about 5.5 W asleep, 17% of the battery in
under three hours, and dies overnight with the lid closed.

This differs from the ASUS ProArt P14, where the states come enabled but
with a minimum residency of 0xFFFFFFFF, which 1023 keys on. On the 83N7
the residencies are real (DRIPS: 1437 us), so with the states simply
enabled the idle governors would enter them at runtime, which hangs the
machine, as Omarchy found when calling ELST(1) at boot.

On systems with the N1x platform extension (NVDA6210), take the
container states regardless of their Enabled flag and give each an
infinite minimum residency. The governors never choose them;
suspend-to-idle, which picks the deepest state by exit latency, does,
and 1023's wake-latency add still makes DRIPS the deepest. With the
83N7's tables this yields seven states, state 6 being
CoreIdle-OFF+PlatIdle-DSUOFF+PlatIdle-PGKLL+Standby-DRIPS, the same
layout Omarchy verified on the P14.

Signed-off-by: Andrew Murgola <andy@murgo.la>
---
--- a/drivers/acpi/acpi_processor.c
+++ b/drivers/acpi/acpi_processor.c
@@ -1213,7 +1213,8 @@
 				       struct acpi_lpi_state *lpi_states,
 				       unsigned int state_count,
 				       struct acpi_lpi_states_array *curr,
-				       struct acpi_lpi_states_array *prev)
+				       struct acpi_lpi_states_array *prev,
+				       bool n1x)
 {
 	struct acpi_lpi_state *parent_lpi = curr->entries;
 	unsigned int j;
@@ -1227,12 +1228,34 @@
 		struct acpi_lpi_state *flpi;
 		int i;
 
-		if (!(parent_lpi->flags & ACPI_LPI_STATE_FLAGS_ENABLED))
-			continue;
+		if (!(parent_lpi->flags & ACPI_LPI_STATE_FLAGS_ENABLED)) {
+			if (!n1x)
+				continue;
+			/*
+			 * The NVIDIA N1x firmware (Lenovo Yoga 9 2-in-1 16N1X11,
+			 * 83N7) clears the Enabled flag of every processor
+			 * container state (DSU off, package, Standby-DRIPS)
+			 * unless \_SB.PEPD.ELST(1) has set MPEL, which only
+			 * Windows' platform extension calls. Take the states
+			 * anyway, so suspend-to-idle can reach DRIPS.
+			 */
+			pr_info_once("ACPI: N1x: taking the firmware-disabled platform _LPI states for suspend-to-idle\n");
+		}
 
 		if (too_many_states(handle, state_count))
 			break;
 
+		/*
+		 * On the N1x the platform states carry their real residencies
+		 * once enabled, and Linux's idle governors then enter them with
+		 * devices running, which hangs the machine (see the DRIPS note
+		 * in processor_idle.c). Give every container state an infinite
+		 * residency: the governors never pick it, suspend-to-idle, which
+		 * goes by exit latency, still does.
+		 */
+		if (n1x)
+			parent_lpi->min_residency = U32_MAX;
+
 		flpi = &lpi_states[state_count];
 
 		for (i = 0; i < prev->composite_states_size; i++) {
@@ -1264,11 +1287,14 @@
 	acpi_handle handle = pr_handle;
 	unsigned int state_count = 0;
 	unsigned int i;
+	bool n1x;
 	int ret;
 
 	if (!osc_pc_lpi_support_confirmed)
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 
+	n1x = acpi_dev_present("NVDA6210", NULL, -1);
+
 	curr = &info[0];
 	curr->composite_states_size = 0;
 
@@ -1331,7 +1357,7 @@
 
 		/* flatten all the LPI states in this level of hierarchy */
 		state_count = flatten_lpi_states(pr_handle, pr_power->lpi_states,
-						 state_count, curr, prev);
+						 state_count, curr, prev, n1x);
 
 		kfree(curr->entries);
 
